# Background sentinel/heartbeat loops must stay attached to a live Shell task

When arming a delayed wake-up (e.g. "check on this training job in 55 minutes,
then hourly"), `notify_on_output` only works if it is attached to a Shell tool
call that is **still running** when the sentinel is printed, and the sentinel
must be printed to that same call's **stdout** (not redirected elsewhere).

## Mistake made previously (do not repeat)

```bash
nohup bash -c '
sleep 3047
echo "AGENT_LOOP_WAKE_race {...}"
' > /tmp/heartbeat.log 2>&1 &
disown
echo "armed pid=$!"
```

This is broken two ways:
1. The sentinel is redirected into a log file instead of stdout, so no
   terminal ever shows it.
2. The launcher command (the one `notify_on_output` was attached to) exits in
   ~5 seconds after disowning the child — there is nothing left running for
   the harness to watch by the time the sentinel would fire.

Net effect: the wake never happens. The orphaned sleeper silently runs and
writes to a file nobody is watching.

## Correct pattern

Issue the sleep+echo as a single Shell call, let it background naturally
(or force with `block_until_ms: 0`), and put `notify_on_output` on that exact
call:

```bash
sleep <seconds> && echo 'AGENT_LOOP_TICK_<purpose> {"prompt":"<prompt>"}'
```

with `notify_on_output: {"pattern": "^AGENT_LOOP_TICK_<purpose>", "reason": "...", "debounce_ms": 5000}`
on that same tool call. No `nohup`, no `disown`, no output redirection.

Verify after arming by reading the terminal file for that shell id: it must
show no `exit_code`/`ended_at` yet (still running), with the sleep as the
`command`. If the terminal shows an exit code already, the watcher is dead
and must be re-armed correctly before relying on it.

See `~/.cursor/skills-cursor/loop/SKILL.md` for the full pattern (fixed vs.
dynamic schedules).

## Local environment resets silently kill even correctly-armed loops

A correctly-armed loop (plain backgrounded `sleep && echo`, no nohup/disown)
still dies with no error and no notification if the local execution
environment restarts — e.g. the user's machine sleeps/disconnects and
reconnects. Symptoms: the terminal file for the shell id is simply gone
(`ls` the terminals folder returns far fewer files than expected, or a
specific id 404s), and `ps -p <pid>` / `ps aux | grep` for the sleeper find
nothing anywhere on the box. Remote work unaffected by this (e.g. Slurm
jobs, anything running on a separate host) keeps going fine — only the local
watcher dies.

There is no way to detect this proactively (no notification fires when it
happens); a stalled wake-up may just be silence. After any sign of a
possible environment restart (user mentions their machine sleeping/
reconnecting, or an expected wake never arrived), check the terminals folder
and re-arm the loop from scratch if it's gone.
